1
00:00:04,040 --> 00:00:05,330
Good evening, everyone.

2
00:00:05,460 --> 00:00:06,750
Good evening, judges.

3
00:00:07,300 --> 00:00:10,400
I'm Wedny, a designer and developer.

4
00:00:10,420 --> 00:00:13,160
This is Wander, and this is Mila.

5
00:00:13,180 --> 00:00:16,360
Our team is Space Uai.

6
00:00:16,380 --> 00:00:19,440
Our challenge is harmonizing

7
00:00:19,460 --> 00:00:22,680
the satellites that detect

8
00:00:22,700 --> 00:00:25,910
heat spots: MODIS and VIIRS.

9
00:00:25,960 --> 00:00:29,020
At first it may seem like a rather

10
00:00:29,040 --> 00:00:32,140
complex topic, with lots of data, but we'll

11
00:00:32,160 --> 00:00:34,740
show you there's an easier way

12
00:00:34,760 --> 00:00:36,540
to understand all of it.

13
00:00:36,560 --> 00:00:39,180
When we talk about heat spots,

14
00:00:39,200 --> 00:00:42,380
the main topic that comes up is wildfires,

15
00:00:42,400 --> 00:00:44,910
which hit Brazil hard.

16
00:00:46,980 --> 00:00:49,860
Many people may sometimes

17
00:00:49,880 --> 00:00:52,980
wonder how these fires could affect

18
00:00:53,000 --> 00:00:53,980
their own lives.

19
00:00:54,000 --> 00:00:56,560
Today climate change

20
00:00:56,580 --> 00:00:59,900
is a topic being widely discussed, and

21
00:00:59,920 --> 00:01:02,600
wildfires can be a driver of it.

22
00:01:02,620 --> 00:01:04,520
For example, we see that

23
00:01:04,540 --> 00:01:07,600
the number of people with

24
00:01:07,620 --> 00:01:10,320
respiratory diseases keeps rising over time,

25
00:01:10,340 --> 00:01:12,620
all of it tied to changes in the climate.

26
00:01:12,640 --> 00:01:15,320
We also saw that in the Pantanal,

27
00:01:15,340 --> 00:01:18,520
in 2020, 17 million animals

28
00:01:18,540 --> 00:01:20,720
were killed by the fires.

29
00:01:20,740 --> 00:01:26,580
So, beyond knowing and discussing

30
00:01:26,600 --> 00:01:29,560
why this happens, we first need

31
00:01:29,580 --> 00:01:32,320
to track how

32
00:01:32,340 --> 00:01:36,090
and where these fires are, right?

33
00:01:36,860 --> 00:01:39,980
To deal with wildfires, it's crucial

34
00:01:40,000 --> 00:01:41,460
to locate them. Locate them, right?

35
00:01:41,480 --> 00:01:42,840
So we can deal with them.

36
00:01:42,860 --> 00:01:46,160
For that, we use satellites.

37
00:01:46,180 --> 00:01:49,350
We took data from two different satellites.

38
00:01:49,580 --> 00:01:52,740
The MODIS satellite and the VIIRS satellite.

39
00:01:52,760 --> 00:01:55,030
Their scales are very different.

40
00:01:55,480 --> 00:01:58,800
One has a 1 km scale, while the other

41
00:01:58,820 --> 00:02:01,030
has a 375-meter scale.

42
00:02:01,220 --> 00:02:03,960
To give you an easier example,

43
00:02:03,980 --> 00:02:07,040
it's as if one could see

44
00:02:07,060 --> 00:02:10,260
the flames in a neighborhood, and the other

45
00:02:10,280 --> 00:02:12,670
the exact block, with more precision.

46
00:02:13,060 --> 00:02:16,220
And that is exactly our challenge:

47
00:02:16,240 --> 00:02:19,500
reconciling the data

48
00:02:19,520 --> 00:02:22,360
from one with the other, so that

49
00:02:22,380 --> 00:02:25,690
their readings match.

50
00:02:26,000 --> 00:02:29,170
With this data, we built a website.

51
00:02:30,400 --> 00:02:33,660
Our solution is an open platform,

52
00:02:33,680 --> 00:02:36,680
for broad use, from people who are

53
00:02:36,700 --> 00:02:43,900
curious about the topic
to academia and science.

54
00:02:43,920 --> 00:02:46,680
On this site, we combine the data

55
00:02:46,700 --> 00:02:47,760
from these two satellites.

56
00:02:47,780 --> 00:02:51,020
Its main purpose is to keep

57
00:02:51,040 --> 00:02:53,440
a history of all this, and also

58
00:02:53,460 --> 00:02:57,070
to use an algorithm we created,

59
00:02:57,460 --> 00:03:00,040
based on NASA data and some other data

60
00:03:00,060 --> 00:03:01,680
we got from other sources,

61
00:03:01,700 --> 00:03:04,400
to predict areas with

62
00:03:04,420 --> 00:03:07,320
conditions favorable

63
00:03:07,340 --> 00:03:10,770
to wildfires and burns.

64
00:03:11,600 --> 00:03:14,100
Here on screen we have some examples

65
00:03:14,120 --> 00:03:17,380
of the site's views and some insights

66
00:03:17,400 --> 00:03:18,340
it gives us.

67
00:03:18,360 --> 00:03:21,700
We tried to make it so that people

68
00:03:21,720 --> 00:03:23,940
outside the field, who aren't academics,

69
00:03:23,960 --> 00:03:26,570
can also use and understand the site.

70
00:03:27,340 --> 00:03:29,580
So we ask a question,

71
00:03:29,600 --> 00:03:31,140
right? Who uses it, and what for?

72
00:03:31,160 --> 00:03:34,420
As he said, many people can

73
00:03:34,440 --> 00:03:37,980
use this site: fire brigades, civil defense,

74
00:03:37,000 --> 00:03:41,600
city halls, schools,
newspapers. And what for?

75
00:03:41,620 --> 00:03:44,260
To act ahead of time.

76
00:03:44,280 --> 00:03:46,720
Because we were able to map lots of data,

77
00:03:46,740 --> 00:03:48,860
like temperature, wind speed,

78
00:03:48,880 --> 00:03:54,540
wind direction, so we can really

79
00:03:54,560 --> 00:03:55,350
get ahead of it, right?

80
00:03:57,120 --> 00:03:59,000
So basically, the harmonization:

81
00:03:59,020 --> 00:04:01,320
we took the two different datasets,

82
00:04:01,340 --> 00:04:04,060
put them on the same scale, sorted,

83
00:04:04,080 --> 00:04:07,160
cataloged them, and merged them for

84
00:04:07,180 --> 00:04:08,320
better precision.

85
00:04:08,340 --> 00:04:10,900
Then we cross-checked this data with others,

86
00:04:10,920 --> 00:04:14,800
because not every heat source is a wildfire.

87
00:04:14,820 --> 00:04:17,320
So we cross-checked it with burn scar data

88
00:04:17,340 --> 00:04:19,800
and other data, right?

89
00:04:19,820 --> 00:04:20,560
The ones she mentioned.

90
00:04:20,580 --> 00:04:23,520
And we aim to

91
00:04:23,540 --> 00:04:26,640
forecast which

92
00:04:26,660 --> 00:04:29,860
areas are prone to fire spread,

93
00:04:29,880 --> 00:04:32,420
so we can

94
00:04:32,440 --> 00:04:35,480
alert the agencies that will deal with

95
00:04:35,500 --> 00:04:38,600
it and act earlier, which means

96
00:04:38,620 --> 00:04:41,500
spending less money, producing less

97
00:04:41,520 --> 00:04:44,550
smoke, and fewer consequences.

98
00:04:47,250 --> 00:04:50,090
We couldn't show on the slides

99
00:04:50,110 --> 00:04:53,070
every part of our explanation,

100
00:04:53,090 --> 00:04:54,930
but we invite you to check it out.

101
00:04:54,950 --> 00:04:57,340
It's already online, for phone and computer.

102
00:04:59,170 --> 00:05:01,780
Is this data real-time?

103
00:05:04,330 --> 00:05:07,530
No, it's not real-time, because

104
00:05:07,550 --> 00:05:10,550
of how these satellites work,

105
00:05:10,570 --> 00:05:13,890
there's a three-hour delay in transferring

106
00:05:13,910 --> 00:05:17,170
the data, plus another limitation we have:

107
00:05:17,190 --> 00:05:20,450
they don't pass over at fixed times.

108
00:05:20,470 --> 00:05:23,710
There are some times when they pass more

109
00:05:23,730 --> 00:05:26,030
often, but it's not

110
00:05:26,050 --> 00:05:28,650
100% certain.

111
00:05:28,670 --> 00:05:31,670
In fact, some of these satellites

112
00:05:31,690 --> 00:05:33,180
don't collect data every day.

113
00:05:34,370 --> 00:05:37,590
Another question: you said

114
00:05:37,610 --> 00:05:40,310
you used NASA data and others.

115
00:05:40,330 --> 00:05:41,550
What are those others?

116
00:05:41,570 --> 00:05:42,800
That's important to mention.

117
00:05:43,660 --> 00:05:47,100
We also used MapBiomas as support,

118
00:05:47,120 --> 00:05:49,460
because on the site we also included

119
00:05:49,480 --> 00:05:52,760
land cover and land use, so we know

120
00:05:52,780 --> 00:05:55,080
what the fire is burning,
whether it's vegetation

121
00:05:55,100 --> 00:05:56,260
or an urban area.

122
00:05:56,280 --> 00:05:59,620
We also used data from INPE

123
00:05:59,640 --> 00:06:02,320
and NASA POWER, which gives us weather data,

124
00:06:02,340 --> 00:06:04,420
like rain, precipitation,

125
00:06:04,440 --> 00:06:05,110
temperature.

126
00:06:06,560 --> 00:06:09,680
Another thing I wanted to ask is about

127
00:06:09,700 --> 00:06:10,730
the target audience.

128
00:06:10,900 --> 00:06:14,390
What did you put? City halls?

129
00:06:14,700 --> 00:06:18,000
And I saw, maybe you mentioned it

130
00:06:18,020 --> 00:06:19,590
and I missed it, society at large.

131
00:06:20,180 --> 00:06:23,380
Alerting society at a few key points,

132
00:06:23,400 --> 00:06:25,580
especially people close to these

133
00:06:25,600 --> 00:06:26,960
fires and so on.

134
00:06:26,980 --> 00:06:28,720
Is that planned?

135
00:06:28,740 --> 00:06:31,760
And how would you do it?

136
00:06:31,780 --> 00:06:34,460
We did think about it: today, civil defense,

137
00:06:34,480 --> 00:06:36,820
when a very heavy rain is coming,

138
00:06:36,840 --> 00:06:39,800
sends a warning to our phones

139
00:06:39,820 --> 00:06:42,500
so we can protect ourselves and prepare.

140
00:06:42,520 --> 00:06:44,870
So we thought about helping with that,

141
00:06:45,280 --> 00:06:47,560
being a complement to civil defense.

142
00:06:47,580 --> 00:06:50,800
Besides weather, fire would also trigger an

143
00:06:50,820 --> 00:06:53,900
alert on the phone of everyone who may be

144
00:06:53,920 --> 00:06:55,340
near the fire.

145
00:06:55,360 --> 00:06:56,880
Then people can take care of themselves,

146
00:06:56,900 --> 00:06:59,100
drink more water, wear a mask, or even leave

147
00:06:59,120 --> 00:06:59,900
the area.
